lib/test/ - libcgra unit tests
==============================

    test_transport.c  Real serial code on local POSIX PTYs: whole-reply
                      deadline under fragmentation, RX/TX counters on timeout
                      and disconnection, and descriptors beyond FD_SETSIZE.
                      Timeout/malformed reply cases assert no retries or RST
                      bytes and CGRA_ERR_DESYNC from later protocol calls.
                      The high-descriptor test reports SKIP if the process
                      limit is too low. No physical serial port is opened.
                      Mid-kernel failures verify partial vector/scan prefixes,
                      matrix/conv sums, unchanged dot output, stopped-session
                      behavior and the distinction between discovery and has_exec.

    test_kernels.c    Tests only the installed cgra.h surface, linked to the
                      static library: matvec, four scans and convolution vs.
                      scalar arithmetic on eight shapes and v2/v3; in-place
                      vector/scan, rejected overlap, capacities, oversized
                      footprints, empty/NULL inputs, canaries and open errno.

    test_faults.c     GNU/ELF linker wrapping injects allocation/read failures:
                      conv workspace, open, PTY server readiness and EINTR/EIO.
                      No production test hooks; requires a linker with --wrap.

    test_cgra.c   assert.h unit tests driven against the sim: emulator (no
                  FPGA). Covers config-word packing, the ID handshake, the
                  configure/write/run/read primitives, every vector kernel vs.
                  a host reference, the fixed SHL-of-negative path, the dot
                  product, NULL-argument error codes, the NACK retry path with
                  its stats counters, and protocol v3: cgra_exec's tap masks
                  (single, multiple, empty), the fused reset, argument
                  validation, the sim:v2 fallback producing identical results,
                  and the round-trip/byte saving itself asserted as a property.
                  Eight device geometries cover runtime transfer lengths, buffer
                  canaries, invalid masks, cached ID, and legacy API rejection.

CAREFUL when adding a check: CHECK() evaluates its condition exactly once, on
purpose. Most conditions wrap a call that talks to the device, and the earlier
version re-evaluated the condition for its assert() -- which silently ran every
such transaction twice. Keep it that way.

Output is TAP-style ("ok N - ...", a "1..N" plan, a pass/fail summary); the
binary exits non-zero if any check fails.

Run from the repository root (make -C lib -f lib.mk <target>)
--------------------------------
    test                 build + run (assertions forced on)
    PROFILE=asan test    same, under AddressSanitizer + UBSan
    valgrind             run the tests under valgrind (needs valgrind)

Assertions stay live even in a release build: the test binary is compiled
-UNDEBUG so assert() fires regardless of PROFILE.
